    <!-- ============================================================================================
         DWARVEN WAR RAM (TAOM WarRam feature, Ironpass cavalry, issue #515).

         Mesh provenance: both pelts and all eight bardings are skinned to the STOCK VANILLA HORSE
         SKELETON, bone for bone, so Monster.taom_war_ram is a horse_2 shaped base_monster="horse"
         entry and vanilla horse clips bind with nothing authored. See
         Monsters/LOTR/lotr_monster_war_ram.xml for the full rationale.

         SIZE: body_length="100" gives SetInitialAgentScale(1.0) on the MOUNT agent only (the rider
         is untouched), i.e. the ram ships at its AUTHORED size and is deliberately not shrunk.

         This was 75 first, on the reasoning that TAOM dwarves render at ~82% of human height so a
         horse-sized ram would dwarf its rider. That reasoning was backwards: the war goat is MEANT
         to dwarf its rider. The film reference (Dain at the Battle of the Five Armies) is a beast
         taller at the horns than the dwarf on it, with the rider perched high and his feet well
         clear of the ground, and 75 read visibly too small in game.

         The mesh is authored at exactly vanilla horse scale, confirmed by measurement rather than
         assumption: horsepelvis sits at 1.396m in this rig against vanilla horse's declared
         standing_pelvis_height="1.40". So 1.0 IS the artist's intended size. At 1.0 the ram is
         2.39m long and 2.27m to the horn tips against a ~1.47m dwarf, with its back at 1.40m.

         TWO PELTS, NOT AdditionalMeshes: sk_eb_goat_a and sk_eb_goat_b are alternate bodies, so
         they are two separate mount items. They must NOT be combined via <AdditionalMeshes>,
         because a HorseHarness equipped on a mount SUPPRESSES the Horse item's AdditionalMeshes
         (native compositing). That is exactly how the chariot lost its cart when barding was worn.

         family_type is INHERITED as 1 from the horse, which is what carries vanilla's complete
         rider-death, dismount and rider-fall surface. The known trade is that ram bardings also fit
         horses in the player inventory UI and vice versa. creature-mount-authoring.md Phase 2 is
         explicit that this is the right call and that isolation belongs in C#, not in the family:
         the spider tried family 11 and simply had no rider-death surface at all.
         ============================================================================================ -->
    <Item
        id="taom_war_ram_a"
        name="{=taom_war_ram_a}War Ram"
        mesh="sk_eb_goat_a"
        culture="Culture.erebor"
        subtype="horse"
        item_category="war_horse"
        value="950"
        weight="300"
        difficulty="0"
        is_merchandise="true"
        Type="Horse">
        <ItemComponent>
            <Horse
                monster="Monster.taom_war_ram"
                maneuver="75"
                speed="42"
                charge_damage="18"
                body_length="100"
                is_mountable="true"
                extra_health="20" />
        </ItemComponent>
        <Flags Civilian="true" />
    </Item>
    <Item
